{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 /workspace/tools.py read queryformer-paper-v1 --find '5.2.' --lines 65\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"queryformer-paper-v1\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.16548v1\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2609.16548v1\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"f8e114e22e2e8016610748784b98f2ffb59e5611f92ef03689e11f7aed4de3a6\",\n  \"total_lines\": 591,\n  \"start_line\": 222,\n  \"next_line\": 287,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"222: 0.84631\\n223: 0.210241\\n224: 5.2. Ablation Study\\n225: Table 4 reports ablation results; Figure 4 shows per-epoch trends. The degradation order—cross-attention query generation causes the largest drop, followed by cross-attention, sequence attention, and self-attention—confirms that query quality is the primary performance driver. Removing all four mechanisms compounds the loss (Δ=−0.00110\\\\Delta=-0.00110), validating the additive benefit of each component.\\n226: Table 4. Ablation of attention mechanisms (H=4H{=}4, dmodel=272d_{\\\\text{model}}{=}272).\\n227: Configuration\\n228: Best AUC\\n229: Δ\\\\Delta\\n230: Full Model (baseline)\\n231: 0.84606\\n232: —\\n233: - Replace SeqQueryCrossAttn with MLP\\n234: 0.84568\\n235: −-0.00038\\n236: - Remove QueryCrossAttn\\n237: 0.84577\\n238: −-0.00029\\n239: - Remove QuerySeqCrossAttn\\n240: 0.84583\\n241: −-0.00023\\n242: - Remove QuerySelfAttn\\n243: 0.84586\\n244: −-0.00020\\n245: - Remove all four attention mechanisms\\n246: 0.84496\\n247: −-0.00110\\n248: All runs: K=2K{=}2, 4 epochs.\\n249: Validation AUC across training epochs for four ablation configurations: replacing cross-attention query generation with MLP causes the largest drop, followed by removing QueryCrossAttn, QuerySeqCrossAttn, and QuerySelfAttn, confirming each mechanism independently contributes.\\n250: Figure 4. Per-epoch ablation of four attention mechanisms.Validation AUC across training epochs for four ablation configurations: replacing cross-attention query generation with MLP causes the largest drop, followed by removing QueryCrossAttn, QuerySeqCrossAttn, and QuerySelfAttn, confirming each mechanism independently contributes.\\n251: 5.3. Competition Results\\n252: Table 5 shows the final leaderboard of the Industrial Track. QueryFormer ranks 1st with a test AUC of 0.83254. Post-competition, a modest parameter scale-up improved test AUC to 0.832713; due to limited post-competition time, this configuration was not exhaustively tuned and likely remains below the best attainable setting.\\n253: Table 5. Industrial Track leaderboard (top 3), with post-competition improvement.\\n254: Rank\\n255: Team\\n256: Test AUC\\n257: Δ\\\\Delta\\n258: 1st\\n259: QueryFormer (日之光面)\\n260: 0.83254\\n261: —\\n262: 2nd\\n263: RClaw\\n264: 0.83217\\n265: −-0.00037\\n266: 3rd\\n267: load_state_dict\\n268: 0.83145\\n269: −-0.00109\\n270: –\\n271: QF-scaled-post\\n272: 0.832713\\n273: ++0.000173\\n274: 5.4. Scaling Analysis\\n275: Model capacity was a binding constraint in this competition: the 35M-sample dataset demanded representations beyond a single-column architecture. The scaling study asks which stackable axis gives the best AUC-latency trade-off: view width HH, block depth KK, model width dmodeld_{\\\\text{model}}, or training compute. We report packed-attention latency separately because Round 2 imposed tight inference limits.\\n276: Table 6. Latency-aware HH scaling of the packed bridge on 31.35M training samples and 3.48M validation samples.\\n277: HH\\n278: Train Time\\n279: Eval Time\\n280: Eval Throughput\\n281: Latency / sample\\n282: 1\\n283: 43.7 min\\n284: 1.8 min\\n285: 32.2k samples/s\\n286: 31.1 μ\\\\mus\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
